{
  "image_name": "1f10081d22d950d40cfa344ba5674073.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how altered antibody glycosylation may arise during the development of ACPA-producing B cells, likely in rheumatoid arthritis. At the top, a schematic shows that sugars can be added to antibody variable regions by introducing an N-glycosylation motif into the protein sequence, creating sites that carry complex glycans containing components such as sialic acid, galactose, mannose, GlcNAc, and fucose.\n\nThe lower part places this in the context of a germinal centre reaction. Citrullinated antigen-directed B cells interact with T cells, undergo somatic hypermutation, and then recirculate through the germinal centre. During this process, their antibody variable regions become extensively mutated, and some mutations appear to create new glycosylation sites. These glycosylated B-cell receptors are depicted as helping cells pass a “checkpoint escape,” suggesting a survival or selection advantage within the germinal centre-like environment.\n\nFrom there, the selected cells differentiate along two main paths: into class-switched memory ACPA B cells or into ACPA plasmablasts and plasma cells that secrete antibodies. Overall, the figure links somatic mutation, acquisition of variable-region N-glycosylation, and abnormal B-cell selection to the generation of autoreactive antibody-producing cells.",
